= Solution
{parent=a}

A <connection on a vector bundle> is a real-linear map (complex-linear for a complex bundle)
$$
D:\Gamma(E)\longrightarrow\mathcal A^1(E),\qquad
D(fs)=df\otimes s+fDs.
$$
It extends to the <covariant exterior derivative> by $D(\eta\otimes s)=d\eta\otimes s+(-1)^k\eta\wedge Ds$ when $\eta$ has degree $k$. Its square is linear over smooth functions: in $D^2(fs)$ the two $df\wedge Ds$ terms have opposite signs and $d^2f=0$. The <curvature form of a connection> is therefore the endomorphism-valued two-form
$$
\boxed{\Theta=D^2\in\mathcal A^2(\operatorname{End}E).}
$$
In a local frame with $D=d+\theta$, it is $\Theta=d\theta+\theta\wedge\theta$.

Define the <tensor product connection> on decomposable sections by
$$
\boxed{D(s\otimes t)=D_1s\otimes t+s\otimes D_2t.}
$$
The <Leibniz rule> for $D_1,D_2$ makes this well-defined over smooth functions and makes it a <connection on a vector bundle>. Applying its <covariant exterior derivative> again gives
$$
D^2(s\otimes t)=D_1^2s\otimes t-D_1s\wedge D_2t
+D_1s\wedge D_2t+s\otimes D_2^2t.
$$
The mixed terms cancel by the graded sign. Thus **curvature adds on the two tensor factors**:
$$
\boxed{\Theta=\Theta_1\otimes I+I\otimes\Theta_2.}
$$
Equivalently, the mixed terms in $(\theta_1\otimes I+I\otimes\theta_2)\wedge(\theta_1\otimes I+I\otimes\theta_2)$ cancel because operators on the different factors commute. This also proves the <curvature of a tensor product connection> formula in local frames.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Fix the convention that the <Hermitian metric> is conjugate-linear in its first argument. In a <holomorphic local frame> $e=(e_1,\ldots,e_r)$, let $H_{ij}=h(e_i,e_j)$, so $h(ev,ew)=v^\dagger Hw$. Write $De=e\theta$, or equivalently $D(ev)=e(dv+\theta v)$.

Compatibility with the <holomorphic vector bundle> structure means $D^{0,1}=\bar\partial_E$. Since the frame is holomorphic, this forces $\theta^{0,1}=0$. <Metric compatibility> means
$$
dh(s,t)=h(Ds,t)+h(s,Dt),
$$
or, in the chosen frame, $dH=\theta^\dagger H+H\theta$. Conjugate transpose here also conjugates the one-form factors. Taking the $(1,0)$ component gives $\partial H=H\theta$, and hence
$$
\boxed{\theta=H^{-1}\partial H.}
$$
This proves uniqueness and suggests existence. The proposed $\theta$ has type $(1,0)$, and its conjugate transpose obeys $\theta^\dagger H=\bar\partial H$, since $H=H^\dagger$. Therefore $dH=\theta^\dagger H+H\theta$, proving <metric compatibility> as well.

Under a change of <holomorphic local frame> $e'=eG$, we have $H'=G^\dagger HG$ and $\partial G^\dagger=0$. A direct computation yields
$$
(H')^{-1}\partial H'=G^{-1}\theta G+G^{-1}\partial G.
$$
Because $G$ is holomorphic, $\partial G=dG$; this is exactly the transformation rule for a <connection one-form>. The locally defined connections therefore glue to a global <connection on a vector bundle>. **The Chern connection exists uniquely**, and its <local formula for the Chern connection on a vector bundle> is
$$
\boxed{D=d+H^{-1}\partial H,\qquad D^{0,1}=\bar\partial_E.}
$$

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <orthogonal projection> $\pi_S$ is a smooth bundle map, since the <Hermitian metric> and the subbundle vary smoothly. The map
$$
E/S\longrightarrow S^\perp,\qquad [v]\longmapsto(I-\pi_S)v
$$
is independent of the representative, bijective on each fibre, and smooth with smooth inverse supplied by the quotient map on $S^\perp$. Hence it is a natural isomorphism of smooth <vector bundles>. It need not be holomorphic. The <quotient Hermitian metric> is
$$
\boxed{h_Q([v],[w])=h_E((I-\pi_S)v,(I-\pi_S)w).}
$$
It is well-defined and positive definite because $S^\perp$ represents each quotient class uniquely.

On sections of $S$, set $D'=\pi_S D_E$. The <Leibniz rule> makes $D'$ a <connection on a vector bundle>. For $s,t\in\Gamma(S)$, the orthogonal terms vanish in the metric pairing, so
$$
dh_S(s,t)=h_S(D's,t)+h_S(s,D't).
$$
Thus $D'$ has <metric compatibility>. Because $S$ is a holomorphic subbundle, the $(0,1)$ part of $D_E$ preserves $S$ and restricts to $\bar\partial_S$. Therefore $(D')^{0,1}=\bar\partial_S$. The uniqueness of the <Chern connection> in part (b) gives the <projected Chern connection> formula
$$
\boxed{D_S=\pi_S D_E.}
$$
It follows that $A(s)=(I-\pi_S)D_Es$ is a quotient-valued one-form. **The printed target $T_M\otimes Q$ is missing a dual:** the <cotangent bundle> is the correct factor,
$$
\boxed{A(s)\in\Gamma(T_M^*\otimes Q).}
$$
Complex-valued forms use the complexified cotangent bundle here. In fact the $(0,1)$ part cancels, so $A$ is the <second fundamental form of a holomorphic subbundle>, an element of $\mathcal A^{1,0}(\operatorname{Hom}(S,Q))$. Finally the two connection <Leibniz rules> give
$$
A(fs)=df\otimes s+fD_Es-df\otimes s-fD_Ss
=\boxed{fA(s)}.
$$
This proves its <tensoriality> and all the asserted quotient and projection properties.

